course: dictionary
lesson: 13751
title: Interjections: words that show sudden feelings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An interjection is a word that shows a sudden feeling, such as surprise, pain or joy. It often stands alone and is followed by an exclamation mark. «Wow!» tells you the speaker is amazed.
explain: «Ouch!» shows pain, and «Oops!» shows a small mistake. Look at the words around the interjection to check its feeling: «Oops, I dropped my cake» tells you the speaker is sorry or embarrassed, not angry.
explain: An interjection can also break into a conversation. Someone may say «Ahem!» to get attention, or «Hey!» to stop a friend. This is the second meaning: something said suddenly to interrupt someone.
fact: An interjection is one of the word classes, like noun, verb and adjective.
fact: Interjections often end with an exclamation mark (!).
fact: Common examples are ah, oh, phew, ugh, yikes, hooray and ahem.
fact: «Phew!» shows relief, which means a worry is over.
fact: An interjection usually stands alone, and it does not change its form.
exercise: «Ouch! I stubbed my toe on the chair.» Which word shows the sudden feeling of pain? | *Ouch | Stubbed | Chair | Toe
exercise: «Hooray! We have won the match!» Which word is the interjection? | We | match | won | *Hooray
exercise: «Ahem, could I have your attention, please?» Why does the speaker say ahem? | *To get attention by interrupting | To say goodbye | To count to ten | To show it is raining
test: «Phew, the test is over!» What feeling does phew show? | Anger | Hunger | Fear | *Relief
test: Which of these is an interjection? | Happily | *Yikes | Dog | Quickly
exam: «Oh no! The bus has left without me.» Which word is an interjection, and what does it show? | *Oh, showing shock or dismay | Bus | Left | Without
